## Why This Is Asked
Path auto-complete is a common editor feature; it probes practical data-structure trade-offs beyond textbooks.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node children map
- Prefix search via traversal then DFS for completions
- Memory sharing of prefixes and separator handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
class PathTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insertPath(path) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of path) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, maxResults) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (cur, built) => {
      if (results.length >= maxResults) return;
      if (cur.isEnd) results.push(prefix + built);
      for (const [ch, child] of cur.next) dfs(child, built + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, "");
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory with extremely large path samples (e.g., thousands of common prefixes)?
- How would you support ranking suggestions by frequency or recency without breaking O(length) per insert?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply DFS for topological ordering and then DP-style relaxation on a DAG to compute shortest paths without a heap. Also checks path reconstruction via a parent array.

## Key Concepts

- DFS-based topological sort
- DAG single-source shortest path via relaxation in topo order
- Path reconstruction with a parent array
- Complexity: O(n+m)

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(n, edges, start, target) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) g[u].push([v, w]);
  const visited = Array(n).fill(false);
  const topo = [];
  function dfs(u){
    visited[u] = true;
    for (const [v] of g[u]) if (!visited[v]) dfs(v);
    topo.push(u);
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!visited[i]) dfs(i);
  topo.reverse();
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  dist[start] = 0;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u] === INF) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of g[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        parent[v] = u;
      }
    }
  }
  if (dist[target] === INF) return {time: -1, path: []};
  const path = [];
  for (let x = target; x != -1; x = parent[x]) path.push(x);
  path.reverse();
  return {time: dist[target], path};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multiple sources or tie-breaking paths?
- How would you adapt if the graph could contain cycles (cycle detection and fallback to Dijkstra)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of DAG longest-path DP and cycle detection using topological sort, a common scheduling/build scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn's algorithm)
- Longest path in a DAG via DP
- Cycle detection via processed-node count
- Handling multiple prerequisites and parallel tasks

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeEarliestFinish(n, durations, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = durations.slice();
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let processed = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (est[v] < est[u] + durations[v]) est[v] = est[u] + durations[v];
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (processed !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return { est, total: Math.max(...est) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the actual critical path from est and predecessors?
- How modify for weighted edges representing inter-task setup times instead of node durations?
- How to scale and detect cycles efficiently in streaming graphs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to combine a Trie with a per-node balanced BST to support dynamic top-k prefix queries in real time—a common optimization in search autocomplete systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie augmentation**: Each node maintains a balanced BST containing (−count, word) pairs for all words in its subtree
- **Efficient updates**: Insert and delete operations propagate along the word path in O(length × log m) time, where m is the subtree size
- **Tie-breaking**: Lexicographic order is used as a secondary sort key when counts are equal

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.top = new BST();
    this.counts = new Map();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle large-scale deployments with millions of prefixes?
- Can you optimize for memory usage when many words have zero frequency after deletion?
- How would you batch updates to improve throughput in high-traffic scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical algorithm design that blends classic sorts and data-structure choices. It assesses the ability to identify run structures, select appropriate sorting strategies by run size, and implement an efficient, stable k-way merge.

## Key Concepts

- Natural monotone runs detection in O(n)
- Insertion sort for small ranges; mergesort for larger ranges
- K-way merge with a min-heap and lazy advancement
- Stability guarantees and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(a){
  const n=a.length; 
  // 1) detect monotone runs
  const runs=[]; let i=0;
  while(i<n){
    let j=i+1;
    while(j<n && a[j-1] <= a[j]) j++;
    runs.push([i,j]);
    i=j;
  }
  // 2) sort each run
  for(const [l,r] of runs){
    if(r-l <= 32) insertionSort(a,l,r);
    else mergeSort(a,l,r);
  }
  // 3) k-way merge using a min-heap
  const heap=new MinHeap((x,y)=> x.val<y.val);
  for(let idx=0; idx<runs.length; idx++){
    const [l,r]=runs[idx];
    if(l<r) heap.push({val:a[l], pos:l, runIdx:idx});
  }
  const out=new Array(n); let k=0;
  while(!heap.isEmpty()){
    const {val,pos,runIdx}=heap.pop();
    out[k++]=val;
    const [l,r]=runs[runIdx];
    const next=pos+1;
    if(next<r) heap.push({val:a[next], pos:next, runIdx});
  }
  for(let t=0;t<n;t++) a[t]=out[t];
}

function insertionSort(a,l,r){
  for(let i=l+1;i<r;i++){
    const key=a[i]; let j=i-1;
    while(j>=l && a[j]>key){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
    a[j+1]=key;
  }
}

function mergeSort(a,l,r){
  if(r-l<=1) return;
  const m=(l+r)>>1;
  mergeSort(a,l,m); mergeSort(a,m,r);
  // merge [l,m) and [m,r)
  const left=a.slice(l,m); let i=0, j=m, t=l;
  while(i<left.length && j<r) a[t++]= left[i++]<a[j]? left[i-1]: a[j++];
  while(i<left.length) a[t++]=left[i++];
  while(j<r) a[t++]=a[j++];
}

class MinHeap{
  constructor(cmp){ this.a=[]; this.cmp=cmp||((x,y)=> x<y); }
  push(x){ this.a.push(x); this.bubbleUp(this.a.length-1); }
  pop(){ if(this.a.length===0) return null; const top=this.a[0]; const end=this.a.pop(); if(this.a.length){ this.a[0]=end; this.sinkDown(0); } return top; }
  isEmpty(){ return this.a.length===0; }
  bubbleUp(n){ const a=this.a[n]; while(n>0){ const p=(n-1)>>1; if(this.cmp(a, this.a[p])){ this.a[n]=this.a[p]; n=p; } else break; } this.a[n]=a; }
  sinkDown(n){ const length=this.a.length; const element=this.a[n]; while(true){ let child2=(n+1)*2, child1=child2-1; let swap=null; if(child1<length){ if(this.cmp(this.a[child1], element)) swap=child1; } if(child2<length){ if(this.cmp(this.a[child2], swap===null?element:this.a[child1])) swap=child2; } if(swap===null) break; this.a[n]=this.a[swap]; n=swap; } this.a[n]=element; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for external sorting with limited memory?
- How does this approach behave with highly repetitive data, and can you optimize further?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem assesses mastery of graph algorithms on DAGs, including topological sorting, feasibility verification under ordered constraints, and shortest-path computation.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort for DAGs
- DAG shortest-path DP (O(|V| + |E|))
- Feasibility checking with ordered mandatory edges
- Path reconstruction via predecessor pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestConstrainedPath(G, S, T, M) {
  const topo = topoSort(G);
  const idx = (v) => topo.indexOf(v);

  const canReach = (from, to) => {
    if (idx(from) >= idx(to)) return false;
    const reachable = new Set([from]);
    for (let i = idx(from); i < idx(to); i++) {
      for (const edge of G.adj[topo[i]]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(to)) reachable.add(edge.to);
      }
    }
    return reachable.has(to);
  };

  const points = [S, ...M.map(e => e[0]), ...M.map(e => e[1]), T];
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!canReach(points[i], points[i + 1])) return null;
  }

  const segmentCost = (src, dst) => {
    const dist = {}; dist[src] = 0;
    for (let i = idx(src); i < idx(dst); i++) {
      const u = topo[i];
      for (const edge of G.adj[u]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(dst)) {
          if (dist[u] + edge.w < (dist[edge.to] ?? Infinity)) {
            dist[edge.to] = dist[u] + edge.w;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return dist[dst];
  };

  let total = 0;
  const fullPath = [S];
  for (const [u, v, w] of M) {
    total += w;
    const seg = segmentCost(fullPath[fullPath.length - 1], u);
    if (seg === undefined) return null;
    total += seg;
  }
  const finalSeg = segmentCost(M[M.length - 1][1], T);
  if (finalSeg === undefined) return null;
  return total + finalSeg;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of in-place sorting algorithms, particularly the challenging problem of merging sorted subarrays without auxiliary space. It distinguishes candidates who understand the theoretical difference between standard merge sort (O(n) space) and true in-place variants, and reveals familiarity with classical algorithms like the Ford-Johnson merge sort or rotation-based merging.

## Key Concepts

- **In-place merge**: Merging two sorted subarrays within the original array without allocating a separate buffer
- **Stability**: Preserving the relative order of equal elements, critical for multi-key sorting
- **Bottom-up merge sort**: Iterative approach that starts with singleton runs and doubles the merge width each pass
- **Rotation-based merging**: Using cyclic block swaps to interleave elements from two sorted segments
- **Time/space complexities**: O(n log n) average time, O(n²) worst-case due to rotation cost; O(1) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function rotate(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Rotates arr[l..m-1] with arr[m..r-1] in-place
  // Uses block-swap: hold arr[l], shift left block right by one, repeat cyclically
}

function inPlaceMerge(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Merge sorted runs [l, m) and [m, r) in-place
  // Find the smaller run, rotate it into position
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr) {
  // Bottom-up: start with width=1, double each iteration
  for (let w = 1; w < arr.length; w *= 2) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += 2 * w) {
      inPlaceMerge(arr, i, Math.min(i + w, arr.length), Math.min(i + 2 * w, arr.length));
    }
  }
}
```

**In-place example**: arr = [1, 3, 5 | 2, 4, 6], merging runs [1,3,5] and [2,4,6] rotates to [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].

## Follow-up Questions

- How does rotation cost O(n) per merge affect constants and worst-case performance?
- How would you adapt this for linked data structures?
- What optimizations would improve practical performance despite theoretical worst-case?
- How does this compare to in-place quicksort or heap sort?

## Why This Is Asked
Autocompletion is common in editors and apps; this tests a lightweight data structure for fast prefix queries and shows practical space/time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with per-node prefix counts
- O(len(prefix)) lookup
- Space proportional to total characters in dictionary
- Edge cases: empty prefix, missing path

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.count = 0;
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.count++;
    }
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support deletions of words while maintaining correct counts?
- How would you extend to Unicode or larger alphabets while preserving performance?

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicates are common in real data and inputs are often skewed. A 3-way partition quicksort directly handles the middle bucket of equal keys, avoiding repeated swaps of equal elements and preventing degeneracy.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag style) for handling duplicates
- Pivot selection strategies (median-of-three to reduce worst-case risk)
- Introsort/hybrid switching to guarantee worst-case bounds
- In-place sorting with O(log n) recursion depth

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  while (lo < hi) {
    let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;
    const pivot = a[lo];
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (a[i] > pivot) { [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt; // tail recursion on the right side
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the pivot strategy to further reduce worst-case risk?
- How would you adapt this to a non-recursive implementation to save stack depth?
- Compare in-place 3-way quicksort with a stable sort like mergesort in terms of cache efficiency and memory usage.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design both memoized and tabulated DP for a non-trivial partition constraint, reinforcing DP patterns and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with arrays
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Power-of-two check with bit trick

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPartitions(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const isPow2 = (x)=> x>0 && (x&(x-1))===0;
  // Memoized top-down
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const top = (i)=>{
    if(i===n) return 1;
    if(memo[i]!==-1) return memo[i];
    let res = 0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) res += top(j);
    }
    memo[i]=res; return res;
  };
  // Bottom-up
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n]=1;
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    let sumWays=0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) sumWays += dp[j];
    }
    dp[i]=sumWays;
  }
  return {topWays: top(0), bottomWays: dp[0]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for large n or multiple queries on the same array?
- How would you handle large counts and modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow?
